\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{18}{5}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380814 13194139536046 true] [17592186380813 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536046 true] [17592186380813 1041 "field-1" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380813 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380814 13194139536046 true] [17592186380813 1110 #uuid "1cb4b9c6-aefa-5b3f-8689-c3aa57c9961b" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380814 1034 "3.6" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380814 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536046 true] [17592186380814 1111 #uuid "fc41aa19-0ad5-58c6-8e54-3335a4eccb99" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380815 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380816 13194139536046 true] [17592186380815 1089 "q-343699" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380815 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536046 true] [17592186380815 1100 "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380815 1113 #uuid "2b0eca4d-00c5-51c3-8921-eb16a9fcdf6a" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380815 1145 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380816 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380817 13194139536046 true] [17592186380816 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380818 13194139536046 true] [17592186380816 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536046 true] [17592186380816 1041 "field-1" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380816 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380817 13194139536046 true] [17592186380816 1110 #uuid "fa68dacb-a747-523d-bcbc-85d4a281848f" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380817 1034 "8" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380817 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536046 true] [17592186380817 1111 #uuid "1da74498-207a-5cd4-a861-2c26491640bb" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380818 1034 "2" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380818 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536046 true] [17592186380818 1111 #uuid "bffc7f9f-08d4-5413-bf59-1531d65e251e" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380819 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380820 13194139536046 true] [17592186380819 1089 "q-343746" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380819 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536046 true] [17592186380819 1100 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139536046 true] [17592186380819 1113 #uuid "9da4341a-f191-500a-9470-7d945f225a70" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380819 1145 "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[16(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 16D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{8}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nOnly solution II matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139536046 true] [17592186380820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380821 13194139536046 true] [17592186380820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380822 13194139536046 true] [17592186380820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380823 13194139536046 true] [17592186380820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380824 13194139536046 true] [17592186380820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380825 13194139536046 true] [17592186380820 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536046 true] [17592186380820 1041 "selection" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380820 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380825 13194139536046 true] [17592186380820 1110 #uuid "2302b378-5802-5058-bcf9-30aa2205b2a6" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380821 1034 "I and III only" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380821 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536046 true] [17592186380821 1111 #uuid "9ae5dab5-06a2-5869-852c-d9ef68b2e33a" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380822 1034 "I only" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380822 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536046 true] [17592186380822 1111 #uuid "b7026065-3548-50fa-bbed-7e5c56ae972e" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380823 1034 "III only" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380823 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536046 true] [17592186380823 1111 #uuid "ddf01c0d-6d5d-57d2-b15c-8a7bf5c874f7" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380824 1034 "I and II only" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380824 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536046 true] [17592186380824 1111 #uuid "9c008c9d-cab2-5ab5-a86a-4ceb00f3f99e" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380825 1034 "II only" 13194139536046 true] [17592186380825 1103 #edb